    "file": "driver/briefs/rule.forbid.unanswered-exits-from-a-blocker.md.min",
    "lines": [
      "# rule.forbid.unanswered-exits-from-a-blocker",
      "",
      "> **a peer blocker is left by ONE door: an answer. every other exit is forbidden.**",
      "",
      "the answer is a \`.taken.by_self\` that states the repair or the refutation, then \`--as absorbed --that <slug>\`. the extant rules say **what to write** (\`converge-with-reviewers\`) and **that every point owes one** (\`…via-a-taken-per-point\`). this says **you may not go around them.**",
      "",
      "## .the forbidden exits — and which the engine can refuse",
      "",
      "| the exit | why it is not an answer | engine |",
      "|---|---|---|",
      "| 🔴 **edit the code and re-roll** | the reviewer may not re-raise it. **absence of a re-raise is not agreement** | ✅ **refused** — the debt is keyed to the reviewer, so it outlives the edit, and the entrance gate reads it before a round starts |",
      "| an empty or token \`.taken\` | the engine tests that the file exists, never what it says | ⛔ **open** — existence is asserted, never content |",
      "| answer some points, not all | the reviewer drops a point that carries a response and **re-raises one that does not** | 🟡 **partly** — the gate counts **reviewers**, never points |",
      "| let the reviewer exhaust | exhaustion is a budget, not a verdict. to spend it rather than answer is a coast | ✅ **refused** — the debt it already minted outlives its exhaustion |",
      "| \`--as blocked\` | a wall is a wall; a concern you have not answered is not one. 🟡 **the converse does NOT follow** — see below | ⛔ **open** — a halt is legitimate; only you know if you answered first |",
      "| call the re-raise a malfunction | 🔴 **a blocker that returns after you fixed it is a driver error.** check whether you ever told the reviewer | ⛔ **open** — a diagnosis is a judgment, and no gate reads one |",
      "",
      "## 🔴 the engine refuses two exits. it does not retire this rule",
      "",
      "⇒ \`philosophy.entoolment-is-the-pinnacle\`.",
      "",
      "## 🟡 the \`--as blocked\` row is about ONE state, and a driver reads it as two",
      "",
      "it forbids the halt for a concern you have **not answered**, and is silent on one you **have** answered and cannot close. that second state is real: **a \`.taken\` converges an ARGUMENT, never an ABSENCE.**",
      "",
      "⇒ 🔴 **read alone, this row sends such a driver back to re-arrive.** the recognition test and its counter-bound live in \`rule.always.raise-a-blocker-a-taken-cannot-close\`.",
      "",
      "🟡 the two do not overlap. one question sorts every case: *"is there a change I am PERMITTED to make that would close this point?"* — **yes** → this rule binds, write the \`.taken\` · **no** → that rule binds, and the halt is correct.",
      "",
      "## ✅ .the two sanctioned exits, and neither is silent",
      "",
      "1. **the answer** — a \`.taken\`, then \`--as absorbed\`",
      "2. **a human overrule** — they take responsibility for the passage. 🟡 ask only after you have answered what you can; an overrule sought in place of a first answer is a forbidden exit",
      "",
      "test: **"if this blocker never re-appeared, would the reviewer have been PERSUADED — or merely distracted?"** persuaded → you answered · distracted → the concern still stands.",
      "",
      "blocker: a re-arrival with an unanswered blocker from the prior round · a \`.taken\` that names neither a repair nor a refutation · a re-raise diagnosed as a reviewer defect with no prior answer on record · an overrule sought before any answer was written.",
      "",
      "⇒ the worked case: \`…example=the-28-iteration-stall.md\` (ref) · the two ✅ rows in code: \`setStoneAsPassed.ts\` (the entrance gate) · \`getLatestPeerGivensPerSlug.ts\` (the slug-keyed debt).",
    ],
  },
  {
    "file": "driver/briefs/rule.forbid.verbatim-quotes-outside-seeds.md.min",
    "lines": [
      "# rule.forbid.verbatim-quotes-outside-seeds",
      "",
      "> **a wisher's verbatim words live in ONE artifact: their seed. every other artifact states the claim in its own words — and does not quote it, and does not cite it.**",
      "",
      "the clamp on \`rule.always.archive-the-wishers-words-verbatim\` — that rule says where the words MUST go; this says where they must NOT, which is everywhere else: a brief, a rule, an invariant, a yield, a fulcrum, a term's \`.reason\`, a commit body, a PR description.",
      "",
      "🔴 **the ban covers the CITATION, not merely the quote.**",
      "",
      "## 🔴 .why the PATH goes too",
      "",
      "| the defect | what it costs |",
      "|---|---|",
      "| **a phantom path where it matters most** | a brief PUBLISHES; \`$route/.seeds/…\` resolves in no other repo. the reader who most needs provenance cannot follow it |",
      "| **it rots faster than the claim** | a route archived, a seed renumbered, a slug reworded — a dead link beside a live claim reads as though the claim died too |",
      "| **it re-frames the claim as a REPORT** | *"the wisher said X, see …"* makes the brief an account of an utterance. **a rule is a law, not a transcript with a footnote** — it binds because it is true, never because someone said it |",
      "",
      "🟡 **a brief is not the seed's index. the seed inventory is.** to cite one seed from a brief starts a second index no census maintains.",
      "",
      "## .the repair — state the claim, and STOP",
      "",
      "\`\`\`md",
      "👎  > *"only urgent concessions qualify for 'needs increased budget'."* — the wisher (seed S14)",
      "👎  the wisher set the line at shipped harm. ⇒ the words: \`$route/.seeds/…case=S14-<slug>.md\`",
      "👍  the line sits at shipped harm: only an \`urgent\` concession earns more budget, and a \`better\`",
      "    one never does — it is maintenance, and maintenance must not weigh the work down.",
      "\`\`\`",
      "",
      "🔴 **the third form does not name the wisher either.** a law carries why it holds, never who spoke it.",
      "",
      "| when… | then… |",
      "|---|---|",
      "| you would paste \`> *"…"*\` attributed to the wisher | 🔴 the strongest cue. state the claim, and stop |",
      "| you would soften that to a **seed path** | 🔴 it feels careful. the path is the same defect, one hop out |",
      "| you write *"the wisher said"* / *"verbatim"* / *"their words"* | cut the attribution. the claim binds on its own |",
      "| a \`.the litigation\` section asks who argued what | it asks for the **argument and what settled it**, never a transcript nor a link to one |",
      "| a rule or invariant would quote to prove it was ordained | 🔴 a law does not prove itself by who spoke it |",
      "| the quote is *"sharper than any restatement"* | 🟡 that sharpness is the SEED's job. write the sharper restatement |",
      "| you cannot find the seed at all | 🔴 never archived — the parent rule fires first. archive it. then still do not cite it |",
      "| the artifact is **published** | 🔴 there the path resolves to naught. the claim must have stood alone all along |",
      "",
      "## .the boundary — a WISHER utterance, never all quotation",
      "",
      "| a violation | not a violation |",
      "|---|---|",
      "| a wisher utterance reproduced in a brief, rule, invariant, yield, fulcrum, or \`.reason\` | the \`.said\` block of the seed itself |",
      "| a **seed path** cited from a brief, rule, invariant, or philosophy | a seed cited from **within the route that wrote it** — a fulcrum, a yield, the inventory |",
      "| a wisher paraphrase dressed as a verbatim quote | a **published external source** quoted with its citation |",
      "| a wisher quote in a commit body or PR description | a **reviewer's** concern quoted in a \`.taken\` |",
      "| a wisher quote carried into a \`.min\` from its source | a tool's stdout, a code line, a file's content |",
      "",
      "**the two lines that part every case:** 1. does the quoted party have a SEED as their record? yes → restate, never copy · 2. is the artifact INSIDE the route that archived it? yes → cite freely. no → the path turns phantom the moment it publishes.",
      "",
      "## .the extant set — fix forward, no sweep",
      "",
      "extant copies stay until disturbed; repair on the way through. **a bulk find-and-replace is forbidden** — it cannot part a copied quote from a cited one, and it would rewrite the seeds themselves.",
      "",
      "blocker: a wisher utterance reproduced verbatim outside its seed · a **seed path cited from a brief, rule, invariant, or philosophy** · a *"the wisher said X"* attribution offered as the claim's ground · a claim the reader cannot read unless they open the quote it cites · a bulk rewrite of quotes across files.",
      "false positive: the seed's own \`.said\` · a seed cited from within its own route · a published external source with its citation · a reviewer concern in a \`.taken\` · a quote in an artifact whose declared subject IS the transcript.",
      "",
      "⇒ see also: \`rule.always.archive-the-wishers-words-verbatim\` (the positive peer, and the typo/profanity repair its \`.said\` carries) · \`rule.require.archaeology-in-notes\` · \`hazard.published-brief-cannot-cite-the-repo-glossary\` · \`im_an.obsessive_learner.for.domain.invariants\`.",
    ],
  },
  {
    "file": "driver/briefs/rule.forbid.workarounds.md.min",
    "lines": [
      "# rule.forbid.workarounds",
      "",
      "> **never work around a failure. diagnose it to its root cause and fix it there — and when the tool that failed is one you build, the failure IS the defect report.**",
      "",
      "a **workaround** = any change to an input, a declaration, a config, or your own conduct that makes a failure go quiet while its cause stands. forbidden. a dream, a \`.taken\`, or a fulcrum that names one documents the defect and leaves it shipped.",
      "",
      "## 🔴 .the dogfooder",
      "",
      "when the failed tool is the one under construction, there is no "human error".",
      "",
      "| the dogfooder thinks | what is true |",
      "|---|---|",
      "| *"my declaration was stale; I fixed it"* | the tool let a stale declaration exist. that is the defect |",
      "| *"the refusal did its job"* | it fired at the costliest moment — a design defect |",
      "| *"I'll note it as a follow-up"* | the follow-up is this round's work |",
      "",
      "## .the bar — a pit of success",
      "",
      "correct use asks **no questions and meets no friction**: the tool guides its caller, and the right move is the easy move. 🔴 **any friction is a critical defect, fixed immediately** — never a nitpick, follow-up, or dream.",
      "",
      "- the wrong state is unreachable, or caught **once, at the cheapest moment**, with the **exact fix printed**",
      "- soundness holds **by construction**, never by a list a human keeps in sync",
      "- the cure ships with a clamp — red without it, green with it",
      "",
      "## .the test",
      "",
      "> **"did the cause of this failure change, or only my input to it?"** cause → a fix · input only → a workaround. stop; fix the cause.",
      "",
      "| when… | then… |",
      "|---|---|",
      "| you edit a declaration, glob, or config so a tool stops its refusal | why did the tool let that state exist, and refuse when it did? |",
      "| one cause refuses many times — per lane, per file, per run | the check sits at the wrong moment. move it to the cheapest |",
      "| a contract stays sound only while a human keeps a list in sync | the contract is the defect |",
      "| you must read a doc or ask to use your own tool right | the tool does not guide. that friction is the defect |",
      "| you write *"the tool did its job"* about a failure you just hit | 🔴 the sharpest cue |",
      "| the same kind of failure shows up twice | 🔴 the first fix was a workaround |",
      "",
      "blocker: an input, declaration, or config patched while the cause stands · a workaround recorded in place of a fix · a dogfooder's friction with their own tool left unfixed in the round it surfaced · one cause that refuses repeatedly, left in place · a contract that needs hand upkeep, left in place.",
      "",
      "⇒ see also: \`rule.always.entool-the-skills-you-touch\` (learner, this repo — this closes its record-the-workaround door) · foreign, in peer repos: \`rule.require.solve-at-cause\` (ehmpathy/architect) · \`rule.require.clamp-edge-cases\` (ehmpathy/mechanic) · \`rule.require.safe-by-default\` (ehmpathy/ergonomist).",
    ],
  },
]
`;

