    "file": "librarian/briefs/knowledge/kno401.actors.1.role.librarian.[article].md",
    "lines": [
      "# 🧩 .brief.article: \`librarian\`",
      "",
      "## .what",
      "a **librarian** is the actor that **curates knowledge collections**: rather than authoring new documents, the librarian operates on existing ones, arranging them into coherent systems that make knowledge accessible and durable.",
      "",
      "---",
      "",
      "## 📑 scope of operation",
      "- librarians do **not** usually instantiate documents themselves.",
      "- instead, they **operate on documents**: selecting, classifying, preserving, and connecting them within a knowledge system.",
      "- where authors **materialize concepts into documents**, librarians **materialize documents into collections**.",
      "",
      "---",
      "",
      "## 🔬 functions of a librarian",
      "1. **selection** → deciding which documents belong in a collection.",
      "2. **classification** → arranging documents into catalogs, indexes, and metadata systems.",
      "3. **navigation** → providing guides, references, and pathways for discovery.",
      "4. **preservation** → maintaining continuity of documents across time and formats.",
      "5. **contextualization** → situating documents in relation to one another, weaving knowledge networks.",
      "",
      "---",
      "",
      "## 📌 examples",
      "",
      "- **climate change:**",
      "  authors produce an 📖 article (definition), a 🗂️ catalog (models), a 🎬 demo (regional case), and a 🎓 lesson (curriculum).",
      "  the librarian curates them into a collection, classifies by type/domain, adds cross-references, and ensures long-term preservation.",
      "",
      "- **philosophy:**",
      "  authors produce an 📖 article (Metaphysics), a 🗂️ catalog (scholastic indexes), a 🎬 demo (Socratic dialogue), and a 🎓 lesson (curriculum).",
      "  the librarian curates them into traditions, preserves across formats, and builds navigation systems (indexes, headings).",
      "",
      "---",
      "",
      "## ✨ insight",
      "- **authors** materialize concepts into *documents*.",
      "- **librarians** materialize documents into *collections*.",
      "- mechanisms like **articulate, catalogize, demonstrate, lessonize** operate **within documents**.",
      "- the **librarian** operates **between documents**, turning scattered works into a coherent knowledge ecology.",
    ],
  },
  {
    "file": "librarian/briefs/rule.always.boot-an-oversized-subdomain-as-its-catalog.md.min",
    "lines": [
      "# rule.always.boot-an-oversized-subdomain-as-its-catalog",
      "",
      "> **when an enbriefed subdomain is too large to boot whole, boot its CATALOG at \`say\` and list every",
      "> member under \`not\`. each member stays reffable via a path the catalog lets a reader compute.**",
      "",
      "\`\`\`yaml",
      "say:",
      "  - briefs/domain.terms/catalog.of=term._.md     # every name + the path formula",
      "not:",
      "  - briefs/domain.terms/term=*.md                # members: read on demand, never booted",
      "\`\`\`",
      "",
      "## .the test",
      "",
      "> **would a reader rather see every member's path, or one index that names them all?**",
      "",
      "| when… | then… |",
      "|---|---|",
      "| a boot spec globs ~20+ files of one subdomain | the strongest cue. catalog it |",
      "| \`rhx cost\` ranks \`<briefs.ref>\` or \`<also>\` near the top | the path list is the cost |",
      "| a boot spec runs over budget | cut paths before cues — they are the cheapest tokens |",
      "| a subdomain begins to fill a directory | write its catalog at the second member, not the fiftieth |",
      "| members are named by coordinate (\`term=$name\`) | state the path formula once |",
      "",
      "the catalog owes: every member by name, grouped by boundary root · the path formula, once, with an",
      "example · a census note (hand-kept; a member on disk absent here is drift) · its rules by path.",
      "it obeys \`rule.require.catalog-is-an-index\`.",
      "",
      "blocker: a catalog at \`say\` whose members are left out of \`not\`.",
      "nitpick: a catalogable subdomain of ~20+ members booted as paths · a catalog that prints every path",
      "where one formula would compute it.",
      "false positive: a handful of files at \`ref\` · a subdomain every boot needs whole.",
      "",
      "⇒ see also: \`rule.require.catalog-is-an-index\` · \`rule.prefer.rules-that-refout\` ·",
      "\`rule.forbid.itemization-without-coordinates\` · \`rule.require.summary-at-the-cluster-root\`.",
    ],
  },
  {
    "file": "librarian/briefs/rule.forbid.brackets-in-filenames.md.min",
    "lines": [
      "# rule.forbid.brackets-in-filenames",
      "",
      "no filename may carry \`[\` or \`]\`. a document kind is marked with the **coordinate form**:",
      "",
      "\`\`\`",
      "👍  kno601.inventories._.kind=article.md",
      "👎  kno601.inventories._.[article].md",
      "\`\`\`",
      "",
      "🟡 **this binds new and renamed files in this repo.** an extant bracketed name in a dependency's published briefs is left in place until disturbed, and re-seeded to the repo that owns it.",
      "",
      "## .why — the marker made the file hard to reference, which is a filename's one job",
      "",
      "| the tool | what brackets cost it |",
      "|---|---|",
      "| \`Glob\` / \`fast-glob\` / \`globby\` | a bracketed name is **unmatched** — the pattern silently returns fewer files than the disk holds |",
      "| \`boot.yml\` glob entries | a \`ref:\` glob cannot address a bracketed brief |",
      "| shell | needs \`\\[\` escapes; unescaped it expands to garbage |",
      "| \`mvsafe\` / \`cpsafe\` / \`rmsafe\` | this repo's own wrappers carry a \`--literal\` flag **that exists solely for this** |",
      "| a markdown link | \`[text](url)\` is link syntax, so \`[article]\` inside one renders wrong |",
      "",
      "## .why \`kind=\` and not a bare suffix",
      "",
      "- **computable** — know the archetype, know the text to search for",
      "- **globbable** — \`*.kind=article.md\` returns the archetype; \`*.[article].md\` returns none",
      "- **self-declared** — \`kind=\` names the axis, where bare \`.article.\` reads as prose",
      "",
      "| when… | then… |",
      "|---|---|",
      "| you name a **new** document with an archetype | write \`kind=$archetype\` |",
      "| you **rename** or move a bracketed file you own | take the coordinate form on the way through |",
      "| you copy a filename shape from a **dependency** | 🔴 that tree's convention is not this tree's |",
      "| you must pass \`--literal\` or \`\\[\` to touch a file | the escape is the smell; the name is the defect |",
      "| a glob returns **fewer** files than expected | check for brackets before you doubt the glob |",
      "",
      "## 🔴 .drop the marker when the lead segment already carries it",
      "",
      "\`\`\`",
      "👍  philosophy.entoolment-is-the-pinnacle._.md   # not …kind=philosophy.md",
      "👍  howto.curate-an-inventory.md                 # not …kind=guide.md",
      "\`\`\`",
      "",
      "a name that **opens** with its archetype does not repeat it. **so the repair for \`howto.$topic.[guide].md\` is to DROP the marker, never to convert it.**",
      "",
      "⇒ the \`._.\` cluster root stays — that marks a **split subject**, a different axis.",
      "",
      "🟡 **a bulk rename cannot fix these safely.** a find-and-replace cannot part a **citation** of a bad name from an **instance** of one, so any sweep would rewrite the citations too. the repair is **file-first and by hand** — one more cost the bracket form imposes, and it lands on the cleanup.",
      "",
      "## .the boundary",
      "",
      "it governs **filenames**. brackets in **prose** are untouched — a markdown link, a table cell, a code block, a citation of another repo's bracketed path. **the defect is a bracket a tool must parse as a path.**",
      "",
      "blocker: a new or renamed filename with \`[\` or \`]\` · a bracketed name copied from a dependency (re-seed the convention instead).",
      "not a violation: an extant bracketed name in a dependency's briefs · a bracket in prose, a link, or a code block.",
      "",
      "⇒ see also: \`rule.forbid.itemization-without-coordinates\` (the contract this reuses) · \`rule.always.enskill-the-tactics-you-discover\` (learner) — check the pavement, **and check it is not a rut**.",
    ],
  },
  {
    "file": "librarian/briefs/rule.forbid.itemization-without-coordinates.md.min",
    "lines": [
      "# rule.forbid.itemization-without-coordinates",
      "",
      "> **whenever you itemize an enumerable concept, the artifact is named by its coordinates.**",
      "",
      "every segment is a \`$key=$value\` pair. a bare word is a violation.",
      "",
      "\`\`\`",
      "$kind.of=$concept._.md                      # the SUMMARY — the axes, the counts, the gaps",
      "$kind.of=$concept.$dimension=$position.md   # one ENTRY per member — always, never optional",
      "\`\`\`",
      "",
      "🟡 **the artifact KIND does not change the convention.** an inventory and a catalog are both itemizations, and **both take this one name.** the kind decides what each entry *carries*; the coordinates decide where each entry *sits*:",
      "",
      "\`\`\`",
      "inventory.of=fulcrums._.md              catalog.of=archetype._.md",
      "inventory.of=fulcrums.case=F14-slug.md  catalog.of=archetype.kind=article.md",
      "\`\`\`",
      "",
      "**both files are owed, always.**",
      "",
      "⇒ it composes two extant conventions rather than a third coinage: \`._.md\` from \`rule.require.summary-at-the-cluster-root\`, and \`$dimension=$position\` from here. 🟡 **\`example=$id\` is the same form**, already in use by \`term=$x._.choice.example=$id\` — one discipline, never three that rhyme.",
      "",
      "## .the case dimension — occurrences",
      "",
      "when members are occurrences rather than product cells — a fulcrum, an incident, a review round — the dimension is \`case\`. 🟡 **an ordinal is not an id:**",
      "",
      "\`\`\`",
      "👎  case=F14                                        # F14 of WHAT?",
      "👍  case=F14-entoolment-ladder-is-fluid-rigid-solid",
      "\`\`\`",
      "",
      "🟡 **join them with a hyphen, never a dot** — a dot is the segment separator, so \`case=F14.slug\` parses as a bare position.",
      "",
      "## .the test",
      "",
      "> **"am i about to itemize an enumerable concept?"** — yes → **the name is coordinates, and both files are owed.**",
      "",
      "🟡 **the trap: the KIND question feels like the NAME question, and is not.** a reader who settles *"inventory or catalog?"* feels finished and reaches for a name freely — which is how \`register.md\` and \`fulcrums.md\` get written. **the name is coordinates in every branch**, so it can be settled before the kind is.",
      "",
      "| when… | then… |",
      "|---|---|",
      "| you name an entry after **what it is about** | 🔴 name it after **where it sits** |",
      "| a filename holds a value with no \`$dimension=\` | the axis is implied; make it explicit |",
      "| you write a summary and stop | 🔴 the entries are owed too |",
      "| you write entries and stop | 🔴 the summary is owed too |",
      "| you reach for \`1.\`, \`2.\`, \`3.\` to order entries | ordinals are not coordinates — order belongs in the summary |",
      "| two entries list dimensions in **different order** | the path stops to be computable — fix one order |",
      "| a cell **cannot exist** | it is forbidden, and it **still gets its file**, which holds the invariant |",
      "",
      "blocker: an entry not entirely \`$dimension=$position\` segments · a bare position · a summary with no entries · entries with no summary · inconsistent dimension order · ordinal prefixes · a **bare-ordinal** \`case=\` with no slug · a forbidden cell with no file · an itemization under a prose name (\`catalog.md\`, \`register.md\`, \`fulcrums.md\`) · an ejected member named by aught but a coordinate.",
      "",
      "⇒ see also: \`rule.require.summary-at-the-cluster-root\` · \`rule.require.inventory-for-enumerable-concepts\` · \`rule.require.catalog-is-an-index\` · \`howto.curate-an-inventory\` · \`kno601.inventories._.kind=article\`.",
    ],
  },
  {
    "file": "librarian/briefs/rule.prefer.decompose-a-subject-via-suffixes.md.min",
    "lines": [
      "# rule.prefer.decompose-a-subject-via-suffixes",
      "",
      "> **when a subject grows a distinct sub-claim, EJECT it into a suffixed peer. do not accrete it inline.**",
      "",
      "\`\`\`",
      "$subject._.md            # the root — the whole subject, and an index of its zoom-ins",
      "$subject.$zoom.md        # one zoom-in per sub-claim, a PEER of the root",
      "\`\`\`",
      "",
      "the root then carries a **pointer**, never the zoom's body. \`rule.require.summary-at-the-cluster-root\` governs the **name** once a split has happened; this governs **whether** to split, and its answer is: prefer the split.",
      "",
      "## .why — a zoom-in that lives inline is COSTED UPFRONT, forever",
      "",
      "| the zoom-in is | what a reader pays |",
      "|---|---|",
      "| **inline in the root** | its full body, **every boot** |",
      "| **a suffixed peer at \`ref\`** | one path — its body **only when dereferenced** |",
      "",
      "## .the second why — accretion is what a root does when it cannot eject",
      "",
      "| when… | then… |",
      "|---|---|",
      "| you would add a **\`##\` section** to a booted brief for a claim that stands on its own | 🔴 eject it and leave a pointer |",
      "| a section has grown its **own** subsections, examples, enforcement | it is a brief. give it a filename |",
      "| a reader would want it **once**, not on every boot | the dereference case exactly |",
      "| a section explains **WHY** where the root states **WHAT** | the why is a zoom-in; the what is the root |",
      "| you reach for a **new top-level name** for a sub-claim | 🔴 a peer name hides the parent. a suffix declares it |",
      "| a subject genuinely has one claim | leave it. the rule fires on the **split**, never before |",
      "",
      "## .the suffix names the ZOOM, not the ordinal",
      "",
      "\`\`\`",
      "👎  philosophy.entoolment-is-the-pinnacle.2.md          # 2 of WHAT?",
      "👎  philosophy.entoolment-is-the-pinnacle.more.md       # names no zoom",
      "👍  philosophy.entoolment-is-the-pinnacle.via-routes.md # legible from a directory read",
      "\`\`\`",
      "",
      "⇒ **a suffix is an address; an ordinal is a counter.**",
      "",
      "## 🟡 .a zoom-in is not a fork — it must not restate its root",
      "",
      "⇒ **the pointer is owed in BOTH directions.** the root names the zoom by path and says when to reach for it; the zoom names its root and says it is a zoom of it.",
      "",
      "| the root holds | the zoom-in holds |",
      "|---|---|",
      "| the claim, shortest form that stands alone | one facet, in depth |",
      "| an index line per zoom-in | the worked cases, the counter-argument, that facet's enforcement |",
      "| the WHAT | the WHY, or the HOW, for one part |",
      "",
      "blocker: a sub-claim given a **new top-level name** rather than a suffix (the relation is unrecoverable from the filename) · a zoom-in with **no pointer** from its root or no back-pointer to it · a zoom-in that **restates** its root · a suffix that is an ordinal or a non-name.",
      "nitpick: a self-contained sub-claim accreted inline where a suffixed peer would serve.",
      "false positive: a single-claim brief left whole.",
      "",
      "⇒ see also: \`rule.require.summary-at-the-cluster-root\` (the NAME to this WHETHER) · \`rule.require.rules-are-clusters\` · \`rule.require.catalog-is-an-index\` · \`rule.forbid.itemization-without-coordinates\` · \`rule.always.yield-the-output-not-the-archaeology\` (driver).",
    ],
  },
  {
    "file": "librarian/briefs/rule.prefer.rules-that-refout.md.min",
    "lines": [
      "# rule.prefer.rules-that-refout",
      "",
      "> **a rule carries its CUE and refs out its ARGUMENT. the cue boots; the case for the cue does not.**",
      "",
      "\`\`\`",
      "rule.$directive.$topic.md               # the CUE — say tier, resident",
      "define.$concept.md                      # the ARGUMENT + the demos — ref tier, fetched",
      "rule.$directive.$topic.example=$id.md   # ONE occurrence — ref (rule.require.rules-are-clusters)",
      "\`\`\`",
      "",
      "## .why — two readers, and they arrive at different moments",
      "",
      "| | the cue | the argument |",
      "|---|---|---|",
      "| who | an author, mid-sentence | a reader who disputes the rule, or first learns it |",
      "| how often | every turn | once |",
      "| a late arrival costs | the prose was already written | naught — they were not mid-sentence |",
      "",
      "⇒ **a cue that must be fetched has already lost the round**, and an argument that boots taxes a reader who did not ask. so it is a **placement** decision: a short argument still refs out, a long cue still stays.",
      "",
      "## .the test",
      "",
      "> **does this fire BEFORE the prose exists, or AFTER a reader disputes it?**",
      "",
      "before → the rule, say · after → the define, ref · you cannot say → argument, so it refs. the burden sits on residence.",
      "",
      "## .what each half holds",
      "",
      "| the rule — say | the define — ref |",
      "|---|---|",
      "| the claim, one line | the etymology, and the rejected peers |",
      "| the test that fires it | the worked pair — 👎 beside 👍, at length |",
      "| the cue table — \`when… / then…\` | the measured case, dated |",
      "| the shapes table — one line per detector | the counter-argument, stated fairly |",
      "| \`blocker:\` / \`nitpick:\` / \`false positive:\` | the boundary, argued |",
      "",
      "🟡 **the enforcement block stays with the rule, always** — a reviewer grades in the pass they read, so a fetched exemption list is one nobody consults.",
      "",
      "## .the cues",
      "",
      "| when… | then… |",
      "|---|---|",
      "| you reach for *"the reason this holds is…"* | 🔴 that sentence opens the define |",
      "| a rule runs past its siblings' median | measure its argument share first. the excess is usually the case for the rule |",
      "| a \`define.$x\` no rule cites | an orphan. either a rule is owed, or the define is not |",
      "| a rule has no define and its argument grows each round | the define is owed |",
      "| a define sits at **say** | read the exception. it is argued, never assumed |",
      "| a rule sits at **ref** | 🔴 the inversion. a cue must be resident or it does not fire |",
      "| you would cut a cue to reach a size target | forbidden — \`rule.require.a-cue-is-not-a-claim\`. move the argument |",
      "| a demo renders as a blockquote | it is still a **cue**. do not sort by markup |",
      "",
      "## 🟡 .the one exception — a FORM rendered reflexively",
      "",
      "a define whose subject is the shape every passage takes stays resident: you cannot reflexively render a form you must go fetch. the test is whether the reader must PRODUCE the subject or merely UNDERSTAND it — a form is produced, an etymology is understood.",
      "",
      "## .the failure modes",
      "",
      "| shape | what it costs |",
      "|---|---|",
      "| the **orphan rule** — no define | the argument accretes, and every boot pays |",
      "| the **orphan define** — no rule cites it | never fetched. written for nobody |",
      "| the **inverted pair** — define say, rule ref | the case boots and the cue is fetched |",
      "| the **mixed rule** — both in one resident file | 🟡 the one you actually have, and invisible until measured |",
      "",
      "## .the measurement",
      "",
      "sort each resident brief's lines: **cue** = a table row · a blockquote · a bullet · a \`👎\`/\`👍\` header · an enforcement line. **argument** = each other line.",
      "",
      "**the line that parts every case: would an author mid-sentence use this?** yes → a cue, resident however long · no, it is why they should → it refs out, however short.",
      "",
      "nitpick: an argument paragraph left in a resident brief · a define at say with no argued exception · a rule at ref · a define no rule cites · a rule whose argument grows each round with no define · an enforcement block moved to a define.",
      "blocker: a cue cut to reach a size target — \`rule.require.a-cue-is-not-a-claim\`'s, never this rule's.",
      "false positive: a define at say for a **form**, exception argued · a one-line ground beside a claim · a demo rendered as a blockquote.",
      "",
      "⇒ see also: \`rule.require.rules-are-clusters\` · \`rule.require.catalog-is-an-index\` · \`rule.prefer.decompose-a-subject-via-suffixes\` · \`rule.require.a-cue-is-not-a-claim\` (bhrain/role=learner).",
