  {
    "file": "librarian/briefs/rule.require.catalog-is-an-index.md.min",
    "lines": [
      "# rule.require.catalog-is-an-index",
      "",
      "**a catalog is an index. one row per member, and no narrative about any one of them.**",
      "",
      "it takes the itemization form — \`catalog.of=$concept._.md\` plus one \`$dimension=$position.md\` per member (\`rule.forbid.itemization-without-coordinates\`).",
      "",
      "## .the three grains — the model every clustered artifact shares",
      "",
      "| artifact | grain | holds |",
      "|---|---|---|",
      "| the **catalog** | the **SET** | one row per member: the pattern, its rule, its detect question |",
      "| the **rule** | the **CLASS** | the mechanism, the detect question, ONE worked case, the fix, the enforcement |",
      "| the **example** (\`.example=$id.md\`) | **ONE OCCURRENCE** | the date, the id, the sweep table, the results, the residual |",
      "",
      "🟡 **an occurrence spans two of those grains and belongs to exactly one:** its own example file. that ambiguity is why it drifts into the catalog by default — the row it amends is real, so the record feels at home beside it.",
      "",
      "## .the test — asked BEFORE a paragraph lands",
      "",
      "> **"is this about the CLASS, about the SET, or about ONE OCCURRENCE?"**",
      "",
      "| the answer | the home |",
      "|---|---|",
      "| the **class** — what the pattern is, how it fails, how to fix it | the **rule** |",
      "| the **set** — a classification, a through-line, a named gap | the **catalog** |",
      "| an **occurrence** — a date, a result id, an outcome, a sweep table | \`example=$id.md\` |",
      "",
      "⇒ prose about the SET is **allowed** and is what a catalog is for. prose about one MEMBER is the violation, however true it is.",
      "",
      "## .the repair is an EJECTION, never a trim",
      "",
      "compressed prose about one member is still prose about one member, and it re-grows next round. **move each occurrence record to its own \`example=$id.md\`, and leave the row.**",
      "",
      "blocker: a \`###\` narrative about ONE member added to a catalog · an occurrence record with no \`example=$id.md\` home · a catalog whose narrative exceeds its index (repair by **ejection**, never a trim).",
      "false positive: prose about the SET — a classification, a through-line, a named gap.",
      "",
      "⇒ see also: \`rule.require.catalog-is-an-index.example=declastruct-aws-security-catalog.md\` (~2,950 lines wrapped around a 28-row index) · \`rule.require.rules-are-clusters\` (the same grains, for a rule) · \`rule.require.inventory-for-enumerable-concepts\` · \`rule.always.yield-the-output-not-the-archaeology\` (driver).",
    ],
  },
  {
    "file": "librarian/briefs/rule.require.inventory-for-enumerable-concepts.md.min",
    "lines": [
      "# rule.require.inventory-for-enumerable-concepts",
      "",
      "when a concept's entries are **enumerable** — its dimensions nameable, each dimension's positions closed — its documents are curated as an **inventory**: one file per cell, addressed by coordinates, with every cell accounted for.",
      "",
      "a prose list, a grouped section, or a partial catalog over an enumerable concept is a **defect**, not a style choice.",
      "",
      "## .why",
      "",
      "- 🔴 **absence becomes visible.** an inventory generates its cells from the axes *before* entries are collected, so an empty cell is a gap. **a list can never report absence** — in a list, *"not there"* and *"nobody looked"* are indistinguishable",
      "- **lookup becomes an address.** know the coordinates, compute the path — no grep, no ranked candidates, no judgment",
      "- **it scales without bound.** an inventory is **ref-only** — one address per entry, no preview — so it grows to any size at a fixed cost. that is what earns it the \`ref\` tier",
      "",
      "| when… | then… |",
      "|---|---|",
      "| a doc **enumerates** members of one concept | ask: are the dimensions closed? |",
      "| you have a **grouped list** whose groups are a dimension | the groups ARE an axis — it wants to be an inventory |",
      "| you add the **Nth entry** to a doc that lists N-1 | 🔴 an accretion path. stop and ask if the space is generable |",
      "| a reader must **grep** to find one member | coordinates would remove the search |",
      "| you say *"the possible values are …"* | you just declared a closed dimension |",
      "| sections read \`## $x\` where every \`$x\` is one position | those headers are coordinates in prose form |",
      "",
      "## .the test — forced articulation, on the page",
      "",
      "> **1. "what are the dimensions, and what are the positions on each?"**",
      "",
      "cannot list them → **not inventoriable.** build a catalog or an \`example=\` set; this rule does not apply.",
      "",
      "> **2. "how many cells does that generate, and how many are occupied?"**",
      "",
      "equal → say so. different → **the difference is the gap list, and it is the most valuable output of the exercise.** a doc that cannot answer 2 is a list in an inventory's clothes.",
      "",
      "🟡 answer 2 **in the root's index**, not in your head — an unwritten gap list decays to zero within a round.",
      "",
      "## .what it requires",
      "",
      "1. **declare the axes** and their closed positions in \`inventory.of=$concept._.md\`",
      "2. **one file per cell**, per \`rule.forbid.itemization-without-coordinates\`",
      "3. **classify every cell** — occupied, forbidden (an invariant), or empty (a gap). never omit one",
      "4. **keep the summary an index** — axes, counts, gaps; no per-entry narrative",
      "5. **re-walk on a new position** — a dimension that grows adds a slice",
      "",
      "🟡 **1 and 2 are both owed, always.**",
      "",
      "## .the boundary",
      "",
      "| the concept | the artifact |",
      "|---|---|",
      "| closed + an address suffices per entry | **inventory** — refs only, scales without bound |",
      "| closed + the reader needs each member's shape at once | **catalog** — an inventory **plus a preview per entry** |",
      "| dimensions open-ended | **catalog** of what is known — a census is not deliverable |",
      "| **occurrences on a declared axis** | **inventory** over that axis |",
      "| occurrences sampled, no axis declared | **\`example=$id\` files** |",
      "",
      "### 🟡 "enumerable" does not mean "enumerable in advance"",
      "",
      "the misread costs a real artifact: *"my entries arise one at a time and I could not have predicted them, so this does not apply."* **it applies.** an occurrence dimension — \`case\`, an incident id, a review round — is a real axis whose positions the summary **declares as they arise**.",
      "",
      "⇒ **an inventory does not require its axes be enumerable in advance. it requires its entries be ADDRESSABLE and its gaps CHECKABLE.** both hold: \`case=$id\` computes to one path, and the census is a \`Glob\`.",
      "",
      "🟡 **inventory and catalog are not partial-vs-complete.** both enumerate fully; they differ in **what each entry carries**. the choice is a budget call, never a rigor one: an inventory with previews is a catalog that has not admitted it (and will grow past its budget while still filed as cheap); a catalog with no previews is an inventory with extra ceremony.",
      "",
      "blocker: an enumerable concept as prose or a grouped list · a root that declares no axes · a silently omitted cell (the omission voids the census claim) · open positions (that is a catalog) · an **occurrence** set under a coined name because its axis was not enumerable in advance · per-entry narrative in the root · an inventory whose entries carry **previews** (a catalog filed as an inventory — its budget claim is false).",
      "",
      "⇒ see also: \`kno601.inventories._.kind=article\` (the why) · \`howto.curate-an-inventory\` (the moves) · \`rule.forbid.itemization-without-coordinates\` (the name) · \`rule.require.catalog-is-an-index\`.",
    ],
  },
  {
    "file": "librarian/briefs/rule.require.rules-are-clusters.md.min",
    "lines": [
      "# rule.require.rules-are-clusters",
      "",
      "**a rule is a cluster, not a file that accretes.**",
      "",
      "\`\`\`",
      "rule.forbid.$topic.md                 # the CLASS — lean, always the entry point",
      "rule.forbid.$topic.example=$id.md     # ONE file per occurrence — opt-in, on demand",
      "\`\`\`",
      "",
      "the rule keeps **exactly one** worked case — the sharpest — so a reader grasps the mechanism without a second fetch. every **other** occurrence, and the full record of the worked one (the sweep, the injections, the residual), lives in an example file beside it.",
      "",
      "## .one file per occurrence, never one file per rule",
      "",
      "\`\`\`",
      "👍  rule.forbid.$topic.example=R9-U1.md",
      "👍  rule.forbid.$topic.example=R10-U1.md",
      "👎  rule.forbid.$topic.example=occurrences.md",
      "\`\`\`",
      "",
      "## .the \`.see also\` convention — the opt-in must be discoverable",
      "",
      "an example nobody can find is an ejection that lost the record. so the rule points **down** from its entry point:",
      "",
      "\`\`\`md",
      "- \`rule.forbid.$topic.example=R9-U1.md\`   — the 2026-08-09 sweep, 14 injections, 1 residual",
      "- \`rule.forbid.$topic.example=R10-U1.md\`  — the recurrence after the R9 fix",
      "\`\`\`",
      "",
      "one line each, which states **what makes that occurrence worth an open**. a bare path is a list; a path plus the fact that sets it apart is an index.",
      "",
      "## .the test",
      "",
      "> **"does this paragraph describe the mechanism, or one time the mechanism fired?"**",
      "",
      "the mechanism → **the rule** · one time it fired → \`example=$id.md\` · **no worked case at all** → 🔴 keep the sharpest one; a rule with none is an assertion.",
      "",
      "blocker: a second worked case added to a rule file rather than ejected · several occurrences collapsed into one \`example=occurrences.md\` (the id in the filename is the citation).",
      "nitpick: an example with no \`.see also\` pointer from its rule · a rule with **zero** worked cases.",
      "",
      "⇒ see also: \`rule.require.catalog-is-an-index\` (the shared grain model) · \`rule.forbid.itemization-without-coordinates\` (why \`example=$id\` is an address) · \`template.domain-term\` (learner) — \`._.choice._\` / \`.reason\` / \`.example=\` is this exact cluster, already in use.",
    ],
  },
  {
    "file": "librarian/briefs/rule.require.summary-at-the-cluster-root.md.min",
    "lines": [
      "# rule.require.summary-at-the-cluster-root",
      "",
      "> **when one subject splits into many subscopes, the summary is \`$subject._.md\` and each subscope is \`$subject.$more.md\`.**",
      "",
      "\`._.\` marks a **cluster root**: the file that stands for the whole, as a peer of its own parts.",
      "",
      "## .why — the root must sort first, and it must be addressable",
      "",
      "| property | how \`._.\` gives it |",
      "|---|---|",
      "| **it sorts first** | \`_\` precedes every letter, so the root heads its cluster in any directory read |",
      "| **it is computable** | know the subject, know the path — \`$subject._.md\`, no search |",
      "| **it is globbable** | \`*._.md\` returns every cluster root; \`$subject.*\` returns the whole cluster |",
      "",
      "## 🟡 .the summary is a PEER of its parts, never a parent",
      "",
      "\`\`\`",
      "👍  term=route.stone._.choice._.md          # the root, a peer",
      "    term=route.stone._.choice.reason.md",
      "    term=route.stone._.choice.example=x.md",
      "",
      "👎  term=stone/_.md                         # a directory, when none was needed",
      "\`\`\`",
      "",
      "## .where it is already the convention",
      "",
      "| when… | then… |",
      "|---|---|",
      "| a subject grows a **second** file | 🔴 that is the split. the first becomes \`$subject._.md\` |",
      "| you write \`$subject.readme.md\` or \`.overview.md\` | rename it — the root has a name, and it sorts first |",
      "| you write \`$subject.md\` beside \`$subject.$zoom.md\` | the bare name reads as one more zoom. mark the root |",
      "| you reach for a **directory** for two files | do not — a flat cluster costs less and globs better |",
      "| you write an **itemization** | the summary is its cluster root |",
      "",
      "## .the test",
      "",
      "> **"if a reader knew only the subject, could they compute the path to its summary?"** — no, they would read the directory and judge → the root is unmarked.",
      "",
      "## .what the summary carries",
      "",
      "the **whole**, plus an index of the parts. it does **not** narrate any one of them:",
      "",
      "- what the subject is, shortest form that stands alone",
      "- the axes along which it splits, when it splits along axes",
      "- one line per part, with the fact that sets that part apart",
      "- **the gaps** — the parts that are owed and absent",
      "",
      "blocker: a subject split into parts with no \`._.md\` root (the whole has no address) · a root named \`$subject.readme.md\`, \`.overview.md\`, or bare \`$subject.md\` beside its own parts · a \`._.md\` root that narrates one part (eject it).",
      "nitpick: a directory created for a cluster of two or three files.",
      "false positive: a single file with no parts — the convention fires on the split, never before.",
      "",
      "⇒ see also: \`rule.prefer.decompose-a-subject-via-suffixes\` (the WHETHER to this NAME) · \`rule.forbid.itemization-without-coordinates\` · \`rule.require.catalog-is-an-index\` · \`rule.require.rules-are-clusters\` · \`template.domain-term\` (learner).",
    ],
  },
]
`;

